# Research Topics: Grandmaster Protocol → Webapp Implementation

## High-Level Organization

The Grandmaster Protocol research reveals 8 findings that must be operationalized in
software. Each requires its own research stream before implementation can begin.

---

## A. Pattern Recognition Engine

### A1. Template Instantiation in Software
The report says templates (10-15 pieces with variable slots) are instantiated in
~250ms. What does this mean for UI design?
- How to measure template formation speed in users?
- What granularity of "position similarity" triggers template matching?
- Can we model template slots as parameterized FEN patterns?
- Literature: Gobet & Simon (1998), Gobet & Chassy (2023), CHREST model

### A2. The CHREST Model → UI Architecture
CHREST says: chunk creation ~8s, familiarization ~2s, slot instantiation ~250ms.
Visual STM holds 3 chunks.
- Design constraint: positions should max out at 3 new chunks per screen
- Timing constraint: flashcard exposure should start at ~8s, drop to ~2s, target ~250ms
- What's the optimal UI for chunk-inspection (highlight which chunks exist in a position)?
- Literature: Gobet (2015), CHREST tutorial (Lane & Gobet 2010), Chassy & Gobet (2011)

### A3. Perceptual Intuition Test Design
The 2023 Gobet-Chassy study showed 44% of evaluation variance explained by Elo
at 5-second exposure, with intuition's work done in 3-4 seconds.
- Can we replicate this as a diagnostic in the webapp?
- What's the minimum set of positions for a valid intuition diagnostic?
- How to score "intuition quality" (not just accuracy, but speed + confidence)?
- Literature: Chassy et al. (2023), Kahneman & Klein (2009)

### A4. Pattern Set Selection
The report notes "optimal position selection: which patterns unlock the most chess
understanding per unit of time?"
- What is the minimal viable pattern set (MVPS) for triggering a micro-fold?
- How to compute pattern coverage overlap between positions?
- Can we algorithmically generate positions that isolate specific patterns?
- What are the "leverage point" patterns that generalize most?
- Literature: Smith & Parra (Woodpecker Method), de la Maza (400 Points)
- Existing sources: Chess.com puzzles, Lichess puzzles, Chess Tempo, Chesstempo API

---

## B. Micro-Woodpecker System

### B1. Cycle Timing Algorithms
The protocol uses 3-4 cycles of 20-30 positions at decreasing time intervals.
- What's the optimal cycle decay function? (geometric? exponential? adaptive?)
- How to schedule cycles across sessions (not just one session)?
- What time-control formats map to each cycle phase?
- Literature: spaced repetition theory (Ebbinghaus, SM-2, FSRS)

### B2. Puzzle Selection for Compression
The Woodpecker Method uses 2000 puzzles. The protocol compresses to 20-30.
- Which 20-30 positions maximize compression?
- What's the selection algorithm: manual curation vs. automated filtering?
- How to tag positions by cognitive load (pieces count, tactical depth, novelty)?
- Can we use Stockfish to auto-grade positions by pattern density?

### B3. Progress Tracking Metrics
- "Cycles completed" is too coarse. What granular metrics matter?
- Recognition time (RT) per position, per pattern class
- Accuracy decay curve (how fast does the user forget between cycles?)
- "Fold detection": at what point does conscious calculation drop off?

### B4. Inter-Session Spaced Repetition
- Can we build an FSRS (Free Spaced Repetition Scheduler) for chess patterns?
- How to map chess positions to review items?
- Should patterns repeat at the same interval as cards in Anki?
- Literature: SM-2 algorithm, FSRS (2023+), Wozniak's research

---

## C. Socratic AI Teaching Engine

### C1. Ramesh RB Method in Prompt Engineering
Core principle: "Teaching is not transferring — never give the answer, engineer
discovery."
- How to structure system prompts that enforce Socratic questioning?
- What's the taxonomy of Socratic probes for chess?
  (a) "Which principle are you breaking?"
  (b) "What does your opponent want?"
  (c) "What changed after that move?"
  (d) "What would you do if I told you that move is wrong?"
  (e) "So what?" (Gulko drill)
- How to detect when the user has "discovered" vs. is guessing?
- Literature: Ramesh RB interviews (Studer 2022, ChessBase India, Belenkaya 2026)

### C2. Position-Dependent Question Generation
- How does the AI select the right question for the current position?
- Mapping: position features → error type → Socratic probe
- If user blunders a fork: "What's the relationship between your knight and my pieces?"
- If user misses a threat: "If you were me, what would you play next?"
- Can we pre-engineer these mappings for the MVPS?

### C3. Discovery Moment Detection
- How to detect when a "fold" or "aha" moment occurs in chat?
- Lexical markers: "oh", "I see", "wait", "so that's why"
- Behavioral markers: faster response time, accurate follow-up moves
- Can we score "discovery quality" per interaction?

### C4. Teaching Strategy Selection
- Given the user's Dreyfus stage, which teaching strategy fits?
  Novice: guided discovery + pattern flooding
  Advanced Beginner: Socratic questioning + minimal hints
  Competent: challenge positions + self-explanation
- How to switch strategies mid-session based on performance?

---

## D. Folding Framework Measurement

### D1. Operationalizing "The Fold"
Hassabis's folding is a metaphor. What measurable phenomena indicate a fold?
- Response time curve: exponential drop-off in think time on known patterns
- Accuracy plateau followed by sudden jump (phase transition)
- Self-report: user says "I just *saw* it instead of calculating"
- Piece focus shift: from calculating individual pieces to seeing clusters
- Literature: Hassabis (Lex Fridman 2021), McGrath et al. (2022 AlphaZero PNAS)

### D2. Phase Transition Detection Algorithms
AlphaZero showed phase transitions at ~32,000 training steps.
- Can we detect phase transitions in human learning in real-time?
- What statistical methods? (change-point detection, CUSUM, Bayesian online detection)
- What data granularity is needed? (per-move, per-position, per-session?)

### D3. Dreyfus Stage Classification
The 5 Dreyfus stages map to folding. How to classify users?
- Stage 1 (Novice): slow, rule-based, frequent basic errors
- Stage 2 (Adv. Beginner): recognizes situational aspects, inconsistent
- Stage 3 (Competent): conscious planning, deliberate
- Stage 4 (Proficient): intuitive pattern-driven, mostly accurate
- Stage 5 (Expert): holistic, creative, fast
- Can we classify from behavior? (think time, accuracy pattern, error type, chat responses)

### D4. Learning Curve Modeling
- What function best fits chess skill acquisition? (power law? exponential?
  logistic?)
- How to model inter-individual variance in compressibility?
- Can we predict a user's "folding potential" from baseline tests?

---

## E. Position Engineering

### E1. Algorithmic Position Generation
- Can we use Stockfish + heuristics to generate teaching positions?
- Constraints: minimal pieces, clear theme, one concept at a time
- How to verify a position teaches its intended pattern?
- Literature: Dvoretsky's "positional sketches", Soviet training methods

### E2. Position Complexity Metrics
- What makes a position "simple" vs. "complex" for learning?
- Variables: piece count, material imbalance, pawn structure complexity,
  number of threats, tactical density, king safety
- How to order positions by complexity for progressive loading?

### E3. Pattern Map of Chess
- What's the complete graph of chess patterns and their dependencies?
- Fork → double attack → discovered attack → pin → skewer
- Pawn structure → outpost → open file → seventh rank
- King safety → attack → sacrifice → breakthrough
- If we can build this map, we can sequence learning optimally.
- Literature: comprehensive tactic taxonomy (e.g., Chess Tempo's categories)

### E4. Reduced Position Design
- The report insists on "minimalist positions" (5-8 pieces)
- What's the smallest position that teaches each concept?
- Can we find the "minimum viable position" for each of the 50 core patterns?
- How to verify a minimal position doesn't create artificial/unrealistic scenarios?

### E5. Blindfold Training Positions
- Ramesh has students solve without moving pieces.
- Can we implement a "blindfold mode" where Stockfish evaluates mental moves?
- What positions work best for blindfold training?

---

## F. Psychological Frameworks in UI

### F1. Smirnov's Three Principles as Interactive Drills
- Principle 1 (Attack): position trainer that flags non-attacking moves as suboptimal
- Principle 2 (Activate least active piece): heatmap of piece activity, highlight
  the piece with least mobility
- Principle 3 (Restrict opponent): after each user move, show opponent's best response
  and ask "did you consider this?"
- Literature: Smirnov's Remote Chess Academy materials

### F2. Gulko's "So What?" as UI Interaction
- After every candidate move selection, force user to answer "So what?"
- UI: move tree that shows opponent's best reply after user's candidate
- Track: does the user naturally start doing this without prompting?
- Literature: Gulko & Sneed (2022), Analyzing the Chess Mind

### F3. Kuljasevic's Tri-Currency Evaluation
- Material, time, space as three equivalent axes
- UI: radar chart showing position evaluation across all three
- Ask user to evaluate each dimension separately before deciding
- Literature: Kuljasevic (2021), How to Study Chess on Your Own

### F4. Prophylactic Thinking Training
- Gulko: "What does my opponent want?" every move
- UI: after user selects a move, highlight opponent's best plan
- Score user on prophylactic awareness (did their move address opponent's threat?)

---

## G. Digital Protocol Design

### G1. 60-Minute Protocol → Multi-Session App
The report's protocol is a single 60-minute in-person session. The app needs to
translate this into digital form:
- How to decompose the 5 phases into sessions?
- What session length is optimal for digital delivery? (15 min? 30 min?)
- How to maintain the "board-only" constraint when screens are involved?
- What's lost and gained in the digital translation?

### G2. Phase 1: Priming (Digital)
- How to teach the 3 core principles without a live grandmaster?
- Interactive tutorial vs. AI-guided discovery vs. pre-scripted sequence
- How to verify the user has internalized the questioning framework?

### G3. Phase 2: Micro-Woodpecker (Digital)
- Timed flashcard mode with progressive speed increases
- How to implement the "3 cycles at 10s → 5s → 3s" progression?
- Audio/metronome pacing vs. visual timer vs. automatic advancement

### G4. Phase 3: Template Construction (Digital)
- Timed position exposure (show position, hide, ask questions)
- What questions to ask after each position?
- "What did you see?" → free-text, then AI scores perception quality
- "What's unbalanced?" → multiple choice or open response

### G5. Phase 4: Compression Simulation (Digital)
- Guided game play against Stockfish at adjustable strength
- Stockfish should create teachable moments (not just win)
- How to program Stockfish to play "instructively"?
- After each game, pattern retrospection
- Literature: Kasparov's accelerated training, Red Bull chess immersion

### G6. Phase 5: Integration (Digital)
- Auto-generated review of patterns seen in session
- Identification of "folding moments" (did any occur?)
- Homework: daily 10-second position review for next week

### G7. Protocol Variations
- 15-minute express version (commute-friendly)
- 30-minute standard version
- 60-minute deep dive
- Multi-week program (60 minutes/week for 8 weeks)
- What adjusts? Position count, cycle count, depth of Socratic interaction

---

## H. User Modeling & Analytics

### H1. Pattern Recognition Speed
- Track RT for each pattern type (forks, pins, skewers, discovered attacks)
- Normalize for position complexity
- Produce a "pattern recognition speed profile" per user

### H2. Decision Quality Metrics
- For each user decision, compare to Stockfish evaluation delta
- Categorize errors: tactical (missed calculation) vs. positional (misjudgment)
- Track improvement per error category

### H3. Folding Index
- Composite metric: (speed + accuracy + intuition consistency + verbal fluency)
- How to weigh each component?
- Is folding binary or continuous?

### H4. Long-Term Learning Curves
- How does pattern recognition degrade without reinforcement?
- What's the optimal review schedule?
- Does the micro-fold persist or require maintenance?

### H5. User Archetype Discovery
- Are there distinct learning types? (visual, analytical, intuitive, brute-force)
- Does compressibility correlate with anything measurable?
- Can we predict who will benefit most from the protocol?

---

## I. Stockfish as Teaching Tool

### I1. Evaluation Bar for Intuition Drills
- Show position for N seconds, then show evaluation bar
- User predicts evaluation → compare to actual Stockfish eval
- Score: prediction accuracy by exposure time

### I2. Best Move Confirmation
- User selects intuitive move → Stockfish confirms or refutes
- Track: how often does the user's intuition match the engine?
- Timed: does this correlation improve with the protocol?

### I3. Position Analysis for Pattern Discovery
- After user plays a position, Stockfish highlights critical moments
- Which positions had the highest evaluation swings?
- What patterns were missed?

### I4. Play Strength Calibration
- The protocol says "GM plays at 50% strength"
- How to calibrate Stockfish to play at specific levels?
- Maia Chess (human-like neural net) may be better than pure Stockfish
- Literature: Maia Chess (McIlroy-Young et al. 2020, 2021)

### I5. Lucena (LC0) and Neural Network Evaluation
- LC0's policy network outputs are more human-interpretable than Stockfish
- Can we use LC0 to identify which patterns the user should have seen?
- Literature: Leela Chess Zero, policy network interpretability

---

## J. AI Chat as Grandmaster Proxy

### J1. Grandmaster Persona Modeling
- What makes effective chess teaching personalities?
- Ramesh (Socratic, demanding, engineer positions)
- Polgar (systematic, progressive, flooding)
- Smirnov (essence-based, compression-obsessed)
- Dvoretsky (positional feel, scientific)
- Multiple personas the user can choose?
- How to prompt engineer each consistently?

### J2. Teaching Strategy Decision Tree
- Input: user level, error type, position features, history
- Output: which teaching strategy to use?
- Strategy options: drill, Socratic probe, explanation, demonstration, challenge
- Can this be pre-computed with rules + Stockfish evaluation?

### J3. Error Diagnosis from Chat + Board
- User makes a move → AI analyzes with Stockfish → diagnoses error class
- Error classes: tactical blindness, positional misjudgment, planlessness,
  calculation failure, prophylactic neglect, over-aggression, passivity
- Each class has a prescribed teaching response
- Literature: Gulko's cognitive-behavioral error taxonomy

### J4. Engagement & Motivation
- The Polgar experiment required 5-6 hours/day from age 3.
- How to maintain motivation in a digital self-directed format?
- Gamification vs. intrinsic motivation vs. social features?
- Should the AI track and manage user motivation?

### J5. Session State Tracking for AI
- What must the AI remember across sessions?
- Patterns installed, errors made, feedback given, Dreyfus stage
- How to serialize this state for the AI's context window?

---

## K. Spaced Repetition for Chess

### K1. FSRS Adaptation for Positions
- FSRS (Free Spaced Repetition Scheduler) is the successor to SM-2.
- Three parameters: stability, difficulty, retrievability
- How to map chess position patterns to these parameters?
- Literature: FSRS (Ye & Deng 2023+), Anki SM-2

### K2. Interleaving vs. Blocking
- Blocking: drill the same pattern type repeatedly
- Interleaving: mix pattern types in the same session
- For rapid compression, which works better?
- Literature: Rohrer (2012), Bjork's desirable difficulties

### K3. Difficulty Modeling
- What makes a position "easy" or "hard" to remember?
- Features: piece count, familiarity, similarity to other positions,
  tactical depth, number of patterns present
- Can we learn difficulty from user data?

### K4. Retention Curve Visualization
- Show user how their pattern recognition decays without review
- Motivational: "You've retained 92% of fork patterns"
- Prescriptive: "Review your 10 weakest patterns today"

---

## L. Validation & Testing

### L1. Diagnostic Test Design
- How to measure if the protocol works?
- Pre/post test: 20 positions, 5 seconds each, score evaluation accuracy
- Control group: no intervention, just playing games
- Experiment design for statistical significance

### L2. Metric Validation
- Does "pattern recognition speed" correlate with playing strength?
- Does "folding index" predict skill acquisition rate?
- What's the minimum detectable effect size for a 60-minute protocol?

### L3. Ablation Studies
- Which phase of the protocol contributes most?
- Remove Phase 2 (micro-Woodpecker): what changes?
- Remove Phase 3 (template construction): what changes?
- Can we determine this without a full clinical trial?

### L4. Long-Term Follow-Up
- Do gains persist at 1 week? 1 month? 6 months?
- What's the minimal maintenance schedule?

---

## M. Competitive & Market Research

### M1. Existing Platform Audit
- Chess.com: lessons, puzzles, game analysis, video library
- Lichess: puzzles, studies, analysis board, coordinate training
- Chessable: spaced repetition for openings, courses
- Aimchess: AI-powered analysis, pattern training
- List: what compression techniques do they use?
- Gap analysis: what's missing that the Grandmaster Protocol offers?

### M2. Demand Estimation
- Who wants rapid chess improvement?
- Beginners (under 1200): frustrated, want quick wins
- Intermediates (1200-1800): plateaued, frustrated
- Advanced (1800+): seeking marginal gains
- Which segment has highest willingness to pay?

### M3. Pricing Models
- One-time 60-minute session
- Subscription: weekly protocol sessions
- Outcome-based: money-back guarantee for specific Elo gain
- Literature: de la Maza's 400 Points (proven demand for rapid improvement)

---

## N. Technical Implementation Research

### N1. Position Database Design
- Schema for storing annotated positions with:
  pattern tags, complexity score, template ID, variant slots,
  FEN, PGN, teaching questions, Stockfish eval, time-to-solve
- Search: find positions by pattern, complexity, piece count, theme

### N2. Real-Time Pattern Detection
- Can we use Stockfish to detect patterns in user moves in real-time?
- "User just created a fork opportunity" → trigger teaching moment
- "User missed a pin" → flag for review
- Latency requirement: < 100ms for real-time feedback

### N3. Chat → Board Integration
- The AI chat currently has board context (FEN, move history)
- How to extend this for the protocol?
- AI needs to: set up positions, run timed drills, ask questions,
  evaluate responses, adjust difficulty
- State machine for protocol phases

### N4. Performance Constraints
- Stockfish in Web Worker: is it fast enough for real-time teaching?
- How many evaluations per second needed for pattern detection?
- Can we pre-compute evaluations for the MVPS?

### N5. Mobile Adaptation
- The protocol assumes a physical board
- On mobile, how to maintain the same immersion?
- Touch-based interaction: slower than physical pieces
- Design implications for mobile-first implementation

---

## O. Neuroscience & Cognitive Load

### O1. Cognitive Load Management
- The protocol is intense: 60 minutes of concentrated learning
- What's the optimal session length for digital delivery?
- How to detect cognitive fatigue?
- Adaptations: break timing, difficulty adjustment, mode switching

### O2. Sleep Consolidation
- The report mentions "this learning will continue overnight"
- How to leverage sleep consolidation in digital training?
- Schedule sessions before sleep? Review after waking?
- Literature: Walker (2017), Stickgold (2005) on sleep and skill consolidation

### O3. Interference Prevention
- Learning new patterns can interfere with old ones
- How to schedule pattern introduction to minimize interference?
- Similar patterns should be spaced apart?
- Literature: interference theory of forgetting

### O4. Attention Optimization
- The protocol demands sustained focused attention
- How to design UI that minimizes distraction?
- Full-screen mode, no notifications, dark mode

---

## P. Open Questions from the Report

### P1. Can a micro-fold occur in 60 minutes?
- The report labels this its "weakest evidence" claim
- What empirical test could confirm or refute this?
- How to define "micro-fold" operationally?

### P2. Optimal position sequence
- The report's 8 positions are speculative
- What rigorous methodology could determine the optimal sequence?
- Can we A/B test position sequences?

### P3. Board-only constraint digital equivalent
- In-person: 0 technology, just pieces
- Digital: screen, mouse/touch, Stockfish, AI
- What's gained (instant feedback, data tracking, AI teaching)?
- What's lost (physical piece feel, GM presence, social pressure)?
- Can the digital version exceed the board-only version?

### P4. Individual differences in compressibility
- Some users may respond; others won't
- What predicts compressibility?
- Can we screen users before the protocol?

### P5. Long-term retention without reinforcement
- Even successful folding may decay
- What's the minimum maintenance dose?

---

